# map_to_dictionary.tkr — the G6 additive-bridge fixture. It proves `Map<V>::to_dictionary()` (the
# str-keyed `Map` → generic `Dictionary<StrKey, V>` door unblocked by F3) preserves every entry: a
# `Map<i64>` is populated (including an update-in-place), bridged with `to_dictionary()`, and read back
# through the generic `Dictionary<StrKey, i64>`. The bridge body calls the phantom-owner factory
# `Dictionary<StrKey, V>::make()` and `insert` while `V` is the map's abstract value type-param, and the
# mono pass concretizes them to `Dictionary__g__StrKey__i64` when `Map__g__i64` is stamped — the concrete
# instance is auto-discovered by the instantiation fixpoint's body re-scan (no annotation needed). A
# self-contained project (its own IEq/IHash/StrKey capability corpus). Runs on the C backend.

Feature: map_to_dictionary — the additive Map<V> → Dictionary<StrKey, V> bridge (G6, on the F3 fix)

  Scenario: bridge_preserves_entries — Map<i64> → Dictionary<StrKey,i64> keeps every entry (a=40,b=20,c=12) at i64 and str widths
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 77
